      <p>A <alert>linear isomorphism</alert> is a linear transformation that is one-to-one and onto. We say two vector spaces <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> are <alert>isomorphic</alert> if there is a linear isomorphism <m>T: U \to V</m>.</p>

      <exercise>
        <p>Show that two finite dimensional vector spaces <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> are isomorphic if they have the same dimension.</p>
      </exercise>

      <proof>
        <p>Let <m>\vec{u}_{1}, \dots, \vec{u}_{n}</m> be a basis for <m>U</m> and let <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m> be a basis for <m>V</m>. Then define <m>T: U \to V</m> by</p>
        <me>T(\vec{x}) = \sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i} \vec{v}_{i},</me>
        <p>when <m>\vec{x} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i} \vec{u}_{i}</m>.</p>

        <p>Prove the following claims:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>T</m> is well</p></li>
        </ul>
      </proof>
      -->
    </section>
  </article>
</pretext>
